            <ArticleTitle>Computing aggregate properties of preimages for 2D cellular automata.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>111104</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1063/1.5006143</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Computing properties of the set of precursors of a given configuration is a common problem underlying many important questions about cellular automata. Unfortunately, such computations quickly become intractable in dimension greater than one. This paper presents an algorithm-incremental aggregation-that can compute aggregate properties of the set of precursors exponentially faster than naïve approaches. The incremental aggregation algorithm is demonstrated on two problems from the two-dimensional binary Game of Life cellular automaton: precursor count distributions and higher-order mean field theory coefficients. In both cases, incremental aggregation allows us to obtain new results that were previously beyond reach.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
